                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "◾◾🔹🔷◾ [+1] Good\n[i]A perfectly decent representative of adventure games of that era - good and bad[/i]\n\nYou know these games. A sarcastic and charming protagonist? Check. Funny about the genre conventions, but taking its plot seriously? Check. The lore that becomes near-nonsensical by the end of the game? Check. Every hotspot and item having some witty commentary? Eh, half-check, some descriptions are disappointingly generic, just describing the item. An extremely useful button to highlight active areas to skip the pixel hunting? Check. A troubling signpost-to-moon-logic ratio that eventually forces you to try everything on everything? Check. The ending that feels like it takes a bit too long to reach (~10 hours, just enough to start feeling exhausted by it) yet also feels slightly too sudden? Check. The male and female leads bickering for a while until being separated for half of the game and ending up together in the finale as if they'd been building chemistry the whole time? That's basically a free space.\n\nBut ultimately, the game, despite its flaws, is still fun. It is basically an adventure movie in point-and-click form: globe-trotting, world-saving, grandiose, full of danger and excitement (as much as you can get from a point-and-click game, at least), and it is definitely a great way to spend a couple days.\n\nP.S. On my PC it couldn't start properly until I turned off fullscreen in the launcher settings, so I had to play in a pretty small window, but that's par for the course, and what you sometimes get when trying to play sixteen-year-old game.",
